plan Machines
title   Machines: definitions, states, turns, guards, schedules, activities and reactions
status  proposed
goal
    An author declares an actor's states and events once. Each accepted event produces one
    consistent result; guards, views and tests agree about what the actor can do.
outcomes
    O1  Definitions and machines
        definition(config) and machine(definition, callback) return synchronously and start no
        actors, work, timers or storage. Invalid construction throws one FlowError containing
        all discovered violations in declaration then rule order, with offending field paths.
        The callback runs exactly once with { S, E, O } and returns static machine configuration.

        A definition requires id, states and events. states is an ordered list of leaf names or
        single-key compounds containing child lists. events and optional internalEvents are
        records from event names to Schema field records; {} means no payload. Optional input
        and memory are Schema field records. Nonempty memory requires initial({ input }); empty
        memory forbids initial, and input requires initial. initial runs once for a fresh actor,
        never on restore, adoption or hydration. Original input is unavailable thereafter.

        Optional context maps slots to another definition's Provider.select(selector); context
        binding is @plan.Runtime.O5. Optional operations is a flat record of operation or
        entity-domain handles. Machine configuration mirrors the declared state tree exactly:
        root and compounds require a default direct-child token and states; leaves have neither.
        Nodes may declare on, activities and schedules; only the root may declare onContext.

        Unknown fields, wrong kinds, empty compounds, duplicate siblings, overlapping public and
        internal event names, _tag payload/input/memory fields, self-selecting context and invalid
        operation handles reject. State names is and child, and top-level root, are reserved.
        State paths have at most ten segments. Authored names and ids are nonempty, at most 256
        UTF-8 bytes, contain no C0 controls, DEL or lone surrogates, and retain exact spelling.

        The machine cannot add states, events or operations. Foreign tokens, undeclared handler
        events, foreign producer events, non-public send events and duplicate activity or schedule
        names reject. Targets, operations, outcome events and send/write targets are static;
        callbacks compute values only. Activity parameters may read earlier activities' lanes,
        parents before children; cyclic dependencies reject.

        Definition literals, payloads, context, operation names and state paths infer without
        assertions, explicit generics or wrappers; invalid machine additions are also type errors.
        Definitions expose Event, State, Input, Memory and Snapshot Schemas. Event decodes only
        public _tag envelopes; State accepts complete leaf paths. E.X(payload) validates on every
        call and throws InvalidEvent on mismatch; payload-free constructors take no argument.
        Persisted-value eligibility is @plan.Persistence.O1.
    O2  States and transitions
        Exactly one leaf is active. Substates share the actor's memory, context and lifetime;
        reaching a leaf with no handlers does not complete or dispose the actor.
        S.OPEN.EDITING names that state; token.is(snapshot) includes descendants and narrows
        activity types. token.child(snapshot) returns its active direct child's name, otherwise
        undefined. S.root.child(snapshot) returns the active top-level state.

        on maps an event name to a token, { target, guard?, updateMemory?, actions? }, or an
        ordered array of transitions. target is required and is this machine's token or "self".
        The active leaf's handler is tried first, then ancestors up to the root. At each level,
        the first passing or unguarded alternative wins; all declining falls through to the next
        ancestor. With no enabled transition, nothing changes and no work starts.

        "self" changes no state and restarts no work, including at the root. An explicit target
        re-enters itself if active; targeting an active ancestor re-enters it and its descendants
        through its defaults. For a handler at H targeting T, the unchanged boundary is H when T
        is a strict descendant of H; otherwise it is the nearest proper ancestor of T that also
        contains H. Active states below that boundary exit deepest first; the path to T and its
        defaults enters outermost first. Work at and above that boundary survives unchanged.
        Parents' activities start before children's and stop after them. Defaults reach a leaf
        in one publication with no intermediate work or snapshot.
    O3  Turns
        Input ordering and atomic actor/store publication are @plan.Runtime.O4. An accepted
        transition computes updateMemory before action parameters; both read the pre-turn
        snapshot and event. The Partial<Memory> patch shallow-merges once. Only accepted
        transitions change memory. Updates, action parameters and send mappers receive now, one
        Runtime Clock epoch millisecond value shared throughout the turn; guards receive no time.

        State changes, activity changes, schedule arming and the complete action batch validate
        before publication. One invalid action rejects the whole batch; no valid prefix runs.
        After publication, exited work releases, entered work starts, then finite actions start
        or join. Unhandled or denied events publish nothing, change no revision and start no work;
        denials may be observed in evidence without a revision.

        A pre-commit callback defect preserves prior state, memory, context and store truth,
        starts no work and publishes one issue-only snapshot naming the callback and path.
        It is not retried for that input. This includes guards, updates, action parameters, keys,
        send payloads, schedules, restartOn, activity parameters and when predicates.
        A post-commit mapper, placeholder, optimistic-data or write callback defect publishes
        one issue, enqueues no event and applies no partial write batch; it neither rolls back
        the committed state nor retries that occurrence. Write semantics are @plan.Operations.O5.

        Outside callers can send only valid public event envelopes; invalid payloads or internal
        events fail with InvalidEvent before a turn exists. The same snapshot, event, Clock and
        seeded test randomness produce the same result without consulting host wall time.
    O4  Guards, deny and check
        A guard receives { event, state, memory, context, activities } from the pre-turn snapshot
        and returns true or deny(code, context?). code is a literal string; context is optional
        JSON-safe explanatory data. false is a type error; any invalid return is a guard defect.
        Guards read only these captured inputs and start no work, so views can trust check.

        check(snapshot, event) uses the same handler, alternative and fallthrough rules without
        updating memory, admitting events, publishing, recording evidence or starting work.
        It returns { _tag: "Allowed" } if a transition would run; { _tag: "Denied", reasons }
        if handlers exist but all decline; or { _tag: "Unhandled" } if none exists. Denied reasons
        contain { code, context } for each declined alternative, deepest level then authored order.
        Verdicts compare by Equal.equals. An old snapshot answers from its own captured values.
        Admission lifecycle is a separate concern: @plan.Runtime.O6.
        A defective guard makes check throw GuardDefect without publishing an issue. Internal or
        foreign events are type errors and fail untyped calls with InvalidEvent.
    O5  Schedules
        A node declares schedules: { name: { schedule, send, restartOn? } }. schedule is a
        Duration.Input, an Effect Schedule, or ({ memory, context, now }) => either. An invalid
        duration or schedule prevents entry with the pre-commit defect behavior of @O3.
        send is this definition's payload-free event or E.X.from(metadata); metadata contains
        name, attempt (firings since arming, starting at 1), armedAt, dueAt, firedAt and output.
        Times use Runtime Clock milliseconds; Duration output is undefined.

        Entry arms a schedule; exit cancels it. Descendant changes and "self" preserve deadlines.
        A firing becomes an ordinary event; if its declaring activation ended before handling,
        it is discarded. A Duration fires once; a Schedule fires each step until complete.
        Equal deadlines enqueue by actor admission order, then outermost state, then declaration
        order. An overdue step fires once, never in a burst; the next delay starts at actual firing.

        restartOn({ memory, context }) establishes a silent baseline and leaves the schedule idle
        on entry. After each committed turn, a changed selection under Equal.equals restarts only
        that schedule from its first step at commit time; equal values do nothing. A Duration
        returns to idle after firing, allowing debounce behavior.
        On restore or resume, a Duration preserves its deadline and fires once if overdue; a
        multi-step Schedule restarts at step one. Restored restartOn baselines are silent.
        Hydration activation is @plan.ServerRendering.O5; deterministic time is @plan.Testing.O2.
    O6  Activities and outcomes in machines
        activities: { name: O.x.subscribe(params, { outcomes?, placeholder?, when? }) } runs
        while the declaring state is active and when is not false. params and when may read
        { state, memory, context, activities }. Lane shapes and placeholders are 
        @plan.Operations.O6.
        After each turn, unchanged operation and canonical parameter key keep existing work and
        its original parameters. A changed key releases old work exactly once and starts new work;
        when false releases only that activity. Release failure reports once without blocking
        the transition.

        Finite actions declare optional success, failure, defect, interrupt and unknown outcomes;
        subscriptions declare value, complete, failure, defect and interrupt. Each maps to this
        definition's event: bare for payload-free events, E.X.from(value) for payloads. failure
        accepts one event for untagged errors or an exhaustive error-_tag mapping. Mapped events
        run in later ordinary turns. Omitted outcomes send nothing; facts still publish in lanes,
        and omitted failures or defects publish an actor issue.
        value fires only for a changed confirmed value under Equal.equals, never for predictions
        or optimistic values. unknown carries the Attempt for explicit reconciliation through
        @plan.Operations.O3.

        Lane-only changes publish captured lanes without running handlers, guards, updates,
        actions, schedules, restartOn or onContext. Finite work starts only from an accepted
        transition's actions; operation action semantics are @plan.Operations.O2 and sending is
        @plan.Runtime.O4.
    O7  Context reactions
        Machine-level onContext: [{ select, when?, event }] observes context changes.
        select({ context, memory }) establishes a silent baseline at admission, restore,
        adoption and hydration. Only context waves re-run it, using current memory; memory-only
        changes do not trigger it. Context propagation is @plan.Runtime.O5.
        Equal selections under Equal.equals are silent. A changed selection becomes the baseline
        even if when(current, previous) declines. With no when, or when true, event (bare or
        E.X.from(current)) enqueues after the context turn, in reaction declaration order.
        A select, when or mapper defect publishes one issue, keeps the previous baseline and
        emits nothing; the next wave may try again. Reactions never change state directly.
    O8  The snapshot
        A snapshot exposes state (complete leaf path), memory (decoded value), context (selected
        slots), issues (Diagnostic values), lifecycle (active, disposing or disposed), and
        activities (named lanes, undefined when not running). It has no input, methods, timers
        or public revision. Diagnostic values are @plan.Diagnostics.O1.
        Snapshots are deeply frozen plain data. Reading an old snapshot never consults live lanes.
        Unchanged parts retain identity across publications; Flow-owned containers are copied,
        while opaque application values retain identity.
        A later success or release clears its own source's issue, never another source's issue;
        a new actor incarnation inherits none. Disposal is @plan.Runtime.O6.
verification
    V1  Type tests prove inference and reject undeclared or foreign members and invalid guards.
    V2  Tests prove construction rejects invalid declarations in order without starting work.
    V3  Stories prove self, re-entry, descendant survival and parent/child work order.
    V4  Tests prove pre-turn reads, atomic batches, fixed now, no-ops and callback defect behavior.
    V5  Tests prove check agrees with handling and uses captured snapshots without effects.
    V6  Stories prove schedule order, cancellation, debounce, overdue firing and restore behavior.
    V7  Stories prove activity retention, release, confirmed outcomes and passive lane publication.
    V8  Stories prove reaction baselines, context triggers, event order and defect recovery.
    V9  Tests prove frozen captured snapshots, stable identities and source-specific issue clearing.
questions
    Q1  Should a compound choose its default child from input or memory?
        Version 1 uses static defaults; a different initial child needs an event turn and an
        intermediate publication. Guarded defaults remain open for a demonstrated app need.
    Q3  Deferred to build time
        Internal identity encoding and construction validation machinery.
        Structural compilation of denial codes and warning presentation.
        Schedule stepping implementation and inspection status fields.
        Issue indexing and diagnostic rendering details.
        Type-spike sizing and compiler performance measurements.
        Reflection behavior for proxies, getters, symbols and sparse arrays.
        Future playback and structural-representation extensions.
